# Overview
#
# Technical writers call a word that makes a step sound smaller a
# minimizing word, or a minimizer (`simply`, `merely`, `easily`,
# `straightforward`, `just run`). Each one tells the reader that a step
# is easy or a behavior is small. Readers judge the size of a step for
# themselves, so the word does not add a fact. The fix deletes the word.
# `simply` and `merely` often mean `only` (`requests that merely read
# objects`), so the message offers `only` where the word limits the
# claim. The rule sits at Recommended, because some hits make a risk or
# a speed the point of the sentence.
#
# Expression
#
# `word` lists the minimizers other than `just`, and `neg` lists the
# negations. The first branch of `main` reports a word from `word`
# unless a negation or `more`, `less`, `most`, or `least` comes before
# it. Weir has no lookbehind, so the branch consumes the token before
# the minimizer and checks it against the negations and those four
# words. The filter then narrows the finding back to the minimizer. A
# space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the branch reads a word,
# a space, and the minimizer.
#
# `just` counts only before a verb from `verbs`, a list of imperatives.
# An imperative is the verb form that gives a command, such as `run` in
# `run make` (`just run make`). The second branch reports that `just`
# the way the first branch reports a word from `word`, and its guard
# also skips `than` (`does more than just run the tests`). The first
# branch does not skip `than`, so the other minimizers count after it
# (`more than merely a cache`).
#
# The first two branches need a token before the minimizer, so two more
# branches catch a sentence that opens with `simply` or `merely`. Each
# needs a verb or a glued comma after the word (`Simply run make`,
# `Simply, the cache is a map`). The other words have no opener branch,
# so a sentence that opens with one passes (`Just run make`). For
# `just`, such a branch also fires after a negation (`you can't just use
# a container`), where the word carries the contrast.
#
# `simply` skips a following `put`. The guard sits glued to `simply`, so
# it reads the token right after the word. `Put simply` and `To put it
# simply` count here, because no pattern can look back from `simply` to
# the `put` before it.
#
# `is easy to` and `it's easy to` do not need a guard, because a
# negation splits them (`is not easy to`).
#
# `quickly` counts only after `to`, `you`, or `you can`, and in `quickly
# and easily` (`to quickly set up the project`), where it tells the
# reader that a step is fast.
#
# False Positives
#
# After a negation, the minimizer carries a contrast (`it is not simply
# an offset`), and deleting it flips the claim. After `than`, `just`
# carries the same contrast (`rather than just run the tests`). After
# `more`, `less`, `most`, or `least`, a minimizer states a real
# comparison (`reads finish more easily when the cache is warm`).
# Outside its four branches, `quickly` mostly describes how fast the
# code runs (`the cache fills quickly`).
#
# None of the words needs a guard for a product name or a mention of the
# word, because both are rare in technical prose.
#
# The technical-writing skill bans `simply`, `easy`, and `quickly` only
# in a procedure. The rule also reports a minimizer in a sentence about
# behavior (`the parser easily handles nested lists`), because the word
# does not add a fact there either.
#
# The two opener branches also fire after a negation when a verb follows
# (`is not simply copied`), a case rare enough to leave.
#
# The rule still reports the other minimizers after `rather than`,
# where deleting the word flips the claim too (`a log rather than merely
# a cache`, `rather than simply run the tests`). A `than` guard in the
# first branch passes `a log rather than merely a cache`, but it also
# passes `more than merely a cache`, where the word adds nothing. The
# opener branches still report `rather than simply run the tests` with
# that guard.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseRestatement reports `Simply put`, so `simply` skips
# `put`.
#
# ProseNotOnly reports `just` after `not` and after each contraction
# that ProseNegativeContraction reports other than `can't` (`do not just
# run make`, `don't just run make`), and the `neg` guard keeps this rule
# off that `just`. ProseNotOnly reports nothing else that the `neg`
# guard skips, so `cannot just use`, `can't just use`, `not simply an
# offset`, and `not merely a cache` pass both rules. Without a later
# `but`, ProseNotOnly passes a `not just` that follows a comma or an
# open paren or that opens a sentence, so `read the docs, not just run
# make` passes both rules too.
#
# ProseNotOnly also reports `more than just`, and the `than` guard keeps
# this rule off that `just`, so `does more than just run the tests`
# draws one finding. After any other word, ProseNotOnly passes `than
# just`, so `rather than just run the tests` passes both rules.
#
# `just` has other senses, such as `only` and `a moment ago` (`just one
# entry`, `the file was just created`). Those senses belong to
# ProseJust, which offers a fix for each sense.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 127 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 16 of 20 sampled
# hits told the reader that a step was easy or a behavior small. The
# misfires were `easily` in a warning of a risk (`can easily cause
# errors`), license text, and `quickly` where speed was the point.
#
# The comparison guard dropped 13 hits in the measure.py corpus, and 13
# of 13 compared two things. The `than` guard did not drop any hit in
# the measure.py corpus, so no sample measures it.
#
# The `quickly` branches hit 11 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 9 of
# 11 were minimizers. The misfires made speed the point (`to quickly
# pinpoint where code is`).
expr neg [not, never, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, cannot, couldn't, won't, wouldn't, doesn't, don't, didn't, shouldn't, mustn't, hasn't, haven't, hadn't, needn't]
expr verbs [run, use, add, call, set, pass, type, install, copy, edit, change, create, delete, remove, update, point, drop, put, write, open, click]
expr word [(simply!(( )put)), merely, easily, trivially, straightforward, (easy to use)]
expr just (just @verbs)
expr main [
    <(![@neg, more, less, most, least] @word), @word>,
    <(![@neg, more, less, most, least, than] @just), @just>,
    (simply[(( )<VERB, !put>), (,)]),
    (merely[(( )VERB), (,)]),
    (is easy to),
    (it's easy to),
    (to quickly),
    (you quickly),
    (you can quickly),
    (quickly and easily)
]
let message "Minimizer. Delete the word, or write `only` where it limits the claim."
let description "Flags `simply`, `merely`, `easily`, and similar words that tell the reader a step is easy or a behavior is small."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Flush writes the buffer to disk."
allows "Simply put, the cache is a map."
allows "The offset is not simply an index into the token list."
allows "It isn't merely a cache."
allows "WriteValue cannot easily tell whether the input aliases the buffer."
allows "The migration is not straightforward."
allows "You can't just use a container for this."
allows "The hook does more than just run the tests."
allows "The file was just created."
allows "The cache fills quickly under load."
allows "Reads finish more easily when the cache is warm."
